# Runbook — Competitive / Review-Site Research (source reachability + coverage bounding)

**Status:** live — Last verified: {{DATE}}
**Owns:** which review/competitive sources are actually fetchable, how to extract attributed reviews
from the sources that yield them, and how a NOT-FOUND from a paginated source must be bounded before it
reaches a brief, a decision, or the human.
**Read this BEFORE** any competitive, incumbent-sentiment, or ICP review-research pass
(decision-discipline rung 2 — "research before inventing"). It is the project binding for
{{BENCHMARK_AND_SENTIMENT_DOCS}}.

**Why this file exists.** Reachability is a runtime property of our fetch path — it is not documented
anywhere upstream and cannot be guessed. Without a durable record, every research pass rediscovers the
same hard blocks by spending attempts on them, and every agent re-learns the same extraction technique.
The origin (Auxara Dialer, 2026-08-06): a session burned fetches on two sources that hard-403 us, and a
WebFetch summariser volunteered *"After reviewing all 919 reviews displayed on this page"* for a page
that renders ~25 — a claim that, relayed once, turns a page-1 sample into a whole-corpus absence claim.

---

## 1. Source reachability matrix

**A row is a DATED observation, not a permanent fact** — reachability changes in both directions.
Re-verify per §4 when a row is load-bearing. Seed values below are from the 2026-08-06 verification on
the shared fetch path; confirm them for this project rather than assuming.

| Source | Status | What it actually returns | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| capterra.com | ✅ WORKS | **Full attributed individual review bodies** — the only source that does | Requires the §2 technique; a default fetch returns only the aggregated summary block. |
| softwareadvice.com | ⚠️ Partial | Aggregated pros/cons blocks only — never attributed individual reviews | Directional signal only; cannot source a quote or a reviewer attribute. |
| getapp.com | ⚠️ Partial | Aggregated pros/cons blocks only — never attributed individual reviews | Same limitation. |
| trustradius.com | ❌ BLOCKED | Hard HTTP 403 | Also kills its `?cg=small` company-size filter — see §3. |
| gartner.com/reviews | ❌ BLOCKED | Hard HTTP 403 | — |
| g2.com | ❌ BLOCKED | — | Long-standing. |
| trustpilot.com | ❌ BLOCKED | — | Long-standing. |
| reddit.com | ❌ BLOCKED | — | User voice reaches us only second-hand via teardowns/listicles — label it as such. |

**Do not spend fetch attempts on a ❌ row.** If a blocked source is the only one that could settle a
load-bearing claim, say so and escalate — never substitute a plausible-sounding secondary and present it
at the same confidence. Aggregator/teardown sources remain usable but are **second-hand**: a complaint
reaching us through a teardown is attributed to the teardown, never to the reviewer.

---

## 2. The extraction technique (why a default fetch under-delivers)

A generic *"extract the reviews/quotes from this page"* prompt against a review site reliably returns
**only the aggregated pros/cons summary block**. The individual review bodies are present, but the
summariser collapses past them. Two things are both required:

1. **Paginate explicitly** — append `?page=N` and fetch each page as its own request. Page 1 is not a
   sample of the corpus; it is the most recent/most-promoted slice and systematically under-represents
   the specific operational complaints that make a review useful. *In the origin session the decisive
   quotes came from pages 3 and 5, not page 1.*
2. **Prompt for the specific, not the general** — ask it to **name specific reviewers** or to **print the
   complete Cons text verbatim**. "Extract quotes" or "summarize the complaints" returns the summary block.

Then apply the normal evidence discipline: quote it, attribute it (reviewer + product + source), and
record the row in {{RESEARCH_LEDGER_DOC}}.

---

## 3. Segment targeting after a filter becomes unreachable

When a source's company-size / segment filter is the cleanest lens for your ICP and that source is
blocked, the substitute is **per-review reviewer metadata** (company size, industry, role, usage
duration) on a reachable source, applied *after* fetching — which means it must be applied over the
paginated pages from §2, not over page 1 alone.

When a claim is scoped to a segment, say which reviewer metadata supported the scoping. An unscoped
review corpus does not become segment evidence because the product is sold to that segment.

---

## 4. Re-verifying a row

1. Fetch one representative URL for that source.
2. Record the **actual** outcome — the HTTP status, or what the body contained (attributed reviews vs
   aggregated block).
3. Update the row **in place** with the new date. Do not append a second row beside it and do not add an
   "as of" footnote below the old one — replace the claim (decision-discipline: replace, don't layer).
4. If a source flips from ❌ to ✅, say so in the report; it changes what future research can prove.
5. **These facts are shared across every project on this fetch path.** When you correct a row, correct
   the sibling projects' copies in the same turn (doctrine-loop universal backflow), or they drift into
   disagreeing about the same observable fact.

---

## 5. Coverage bounding — a summariser's completeness claim is NOT the researcher's coverage claim

A fetch summariser reports on **the payload it received**. It does not know the corpus size, it cannot
see the pages it was not given, and it will volunteer confident totality language anyway. Relaying that
as coverage converts a page-1 sample into a whole-corpus absence claim in one step, and the resulting
NOT-FOUND reads as settled.

**The rule.** Every NOT-FOUND, "no complaints about X", "nobody mentions Y", or "this is not a pain
point" derived from a paginated source is **explicitly bounded to the pages actually retrieved**:

> ✅ "Not found in pages 1–5 of an unknown total for <product>."
> ❌ "Reviewers don't complain about this."

- **Name the pages you fetched** and, when the source exposes it, the total. When it does not, say "of an
  unknown total" — that is honest and falsifiable.
- **Never repeat a summariser's totality language** ("all N reviews", "the full set", "across the corpus")
  as your own coverage. If the number is worth citing, attribute it: *"the summariser asserted N; the page
  paginates ~25, so that figure is unverified."*
- **Absence needs more pages than presence.** One page can prove a complaint exists. No number of pages
  proves a complaint does not exist — only that it was absent from the pages read. Scale the paging effort
  to how load-bearing the absence is; when an absence is decision-relevant and you cannot bound it well,
  escalate instead of shipping it.
- **The bound travels with the claim** into every downstream artifact — the brief, the decision row, the
  benchmark doc, the human-facing report. A bound dropped on the way to the summary is the failure, even
  when the research itself was careful.

*Fail-state:* a page-1 sample became a whole-corpus absence claim because a summariser's completeness
language was relayed as the researcher's coverage — and a product decision was made on "nobody complains
about this" when nobody had read past the first 25 reviews.
